 # Octree File I/O Format
 
-Also called: Octree Header Format, OctreeInfo
+Also called: Octree File Format & Header, Octree Header Format, OctreeInfo
 
 ## Overview
-This format defines the ASCII header and binary payload structure for serializing a Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) to disk, enabling efficient loading and unloading of volumetric data [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of persistent storage for CPU-based ray casting by separating structural metadata from voxel data, allowing the renderer to map memory efficiently without parsing complex binary headers for every access [S1, unofficial]. It differs from file-system-based indexing methods (like [Octree-based Space Subdivision with File System Indexing](5866-octree-based-space-subdivision-with-file-system-indexing.md)) by storing the entire octree structure in a single contiguous file rather than distributing nodes across the directory hierarchy [S1, unofficial][S2].
+This format stores octree metadata in a separate text header file containing compression type, grid size, node count, and data position [S1, S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of persistent storage for CPU-based ray casting by separating structural metadata from voxel data, allowing the renderer to map memory efficiently without parsing complex binary headers for every access [S1, unofficial]. It differs from file-system-based indexing methods (like [Octree-based Space Subdivision with File System Indexing](5866-octree-based-space-subdivision-with-file-system-indexing.md)) by storing the entire octree structure in a single contiguous file rather than distributing nodes across the directory hierarchy [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-- **Input**: An in-memory Sparse Voxel Octree structure containing node pointers and voxel data.
-- **Output**: A single file containing an ASCII header followed by binary blocks.
+- **Input**: An in-memory octree structure containing node data and leaf voxel Morton codes [S1, unofficial].
+- **Output**: An ASCII header file followed by binary data blocks [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
